// KQL for Microsoft Defender XDR and Sentinel - borrowed inference and the coordination
// substrate.
//
// DEFENDER VANTAGE throughout. Everything here assumes the agents ran inside your estate,
// which is the Unit 42 case. In the Taiwan case the agents ran on the operator's
// infrastructure and none of this would have fired. Section 4.4 of the paper.
//
// Written after Unit 42's 2 September 2026 account of an AI-assisted intrusion: roughly ten
// hours, 50+ ATT&CK techniques, ending with stolen cloud keys used to repurpose the victim's
// OWN AI endpoints as post-compromise infrastructure.
//
// Author: Cristian Ruvalcaba and the Saluca Agentic AI Research Team, 2026-09-08
// Ref: https://unit42.paloaltonetworks.com/ai-assisted-cyber-attack-inside-a-unit-42-investigation/
// License: Apache-2.0
//
// AUTHORED, NOT EXECUTED. There is no tenant behind these queries. Table and column names
// follow the documented schemas but have not been run. Expect to fix at least one field name
// per query and budget for it.
//
// PREREQUISITE, AND IT IS THE BINDING ONE. Queries 1, 2 and 3 need Azure OpenAI diagnostic
// logging enabled and routed to a workspace. It is OFF by default. If you have not turned it
// on, borrowed inference is invisible in your estate and no query below will help you.


// =========================================================================================
// 1. Model endpoint invoked by a principal that has never invoked it. The P1 detection.
//
// This is the whole borrowed-inference control. It is asked in the control plane, so it does
// not care which provider, which framework, which model, or where on the network the caller
// sits. It survives everything the operator can change for free.
//
// Thirty days of history is the baseline. Shorter and every principal looks novel after the
// window rolls, which turns the query into a noise generator.
// =========================================================================================
let lookback = 30d;
let known =
    AzureDiagnostics
    | where TimeGenerated between (ago(lookback) .. ago(1d))
    | where ResourceProvider =~ "MICROSOFT.COGNITIVESERVICES"
    | where OperationName has_any ("ChatCompletions_Create", "Completions_Create", "Embeddings_Create")
    | summarize by CallerIdentity = tostring(properties_callerIdentity_s), Resource;
AzureDiagnostics
| where TimeGenerated > ago(1d)
| where ResourceProvider =~ "MICROSOFT.COGNITIVESERVICES"
| where OperationName has_any ("ChatCompletions_Create", "Completions_Create", "Embeddings_Create")
| extend CallerIdentity = tostring(properties_callerIdentity_s)
| join kind=leftanti known on CallerIdentity, Resource
| summarize Calls = count(), FirstSeen = min(TimeGenerated), LastSeen = max(TimeGenerated)
    by CallerIdentity, Resource, CallerIPAddress
| where Calls > 5          // a single exploratory call is usually a human clicking something
| order by Calls desc
// TRIAGE: a new workload going live looks exactly like this. That is not a false positive so
// much as a question you should be able to answer in under a minute. If you cannot say which
// team owns CallerIdentity, that is the finding.


// =========================================================================================
// 2. Step change in inference volume per principal.
//
// Catches the case query 1 misses, where the compromised identity is one that legitimately
// calls the endpoint already. Borrowed inference at scale is a resource-abuse pattern and it
// shows up as volume, in the same shape cryptomining does on general compute.
//
// The difference worth internalising: mining is an anomalous workload on general compute.
// Borrowed inference is the EXPECTED workload on the exact service built to serve it. It
// looks like the product working, which is why volume and identity are all you have.
// =========================================================================================
AzureDiagnostics
| where TimeGenerated > ago(14d)
| where ResourceProvider =~ "MICROSOFT.COGNITIVESERVICES"
| where OperationName has_any ("ChatCompletions_Create", "Completions_Create")
| extend CallerIdentity = tostring(properties_callerIdentity_s)
| summarize Calls = count() by CallerIdentity, Resource, bin(TimeGenerated, 1h)
| summarize
      Baseline = percentile(Calls, 50),
      Peak     = max(Calls),
      PeakAt   = arg_max(Calls, TimeGenerated)
    by CallerIdentity, Resource
| extend Multiple = todouble(Peak) / todouble(max_of(Baseline, 1))
| where Multiple > 10 and Peak > 100
| order by Multiple desc
// TUNE: the 10x and the floor of 100 are starting points, not measurements. Nothing here has
// been run against telemetry from the incident, because none is public.


// =========================================================================================
// 3. Secrets read followed by first inference call, same principal, same hour.
//
// The Unit 42 chain compressed into one query: privilege phase into resource-abuse phase.
//
// Read the false-positive note before deploying. An AI workload reading its own provider key
// from Key Vault and then calling a model is a correct and extremely common pattern. The
// exception list IS the deliverable: if you cannot enumerate which identities are supposed to
// do this, you cannot run the rule, and building the list is worth doing regardless.
// =========================================================================================
let window = 1h;
let secrets =
    AzureDiagnostics
    | where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
    | where ResourceProvider =~ "MICROSOFT.KEYVAULT"
    | where OperationName in ("SecretGet", "SecretList")
    | extend Principal = tostring(identity_claim_oid_g)
    | summarize SecretReads = count(), SecretAt = max(TimeGenerated) by Principal, bin(TimeGenerated, window);
let inference =
    AzureDiagnostics
    | where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
    | where ResourceProvider =~ "MICROSOFT.COGNITIVESERVICES"
    | extend Principal = tostring(properties_callerIdentity_s)
    | summarize InferenceCalls = count(), InferenceAt = min(TimeGenerated) by Principal, bin(TimeGenerated, window);
secrets
| join kind=inner inference on Principal, TimeGenerated
| where InferenceAt > SecretAt
| where SecretReads > 10          // bulk read, not a single startup fetch
| project TimeGenerated, Principal, SecretReads, SecretAt, InferenceCalls, InferenceAt
| order by SecretReads desc


// =========================================================================================
// 4. Coordination substrate: structured state files at machine cadence on a server.
//
// HUNTING, NOT ALERTING, and the most vantage-limited query in this file.
//
// The signal is the conjunction, never the file type on its own. Markdown and __pycache__
// are everywhere. What is not everywhere is a service-context process writing dozens of
// structured state files in minutes on a host where nobody develops.
// =========================================================================================
let servers = DeviceInfo
    | where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
    | where DeviceType != "Workstation"
    | distinct DeviceId;
DeviceFileEvents
| where Timestamp > ago(7d)
| where DeviceId in (servers)
| where ActionType in ("FileCreated", "FileModified")
| where FileName endswith ".md" or FileName endswith ".jsonl"
    or FolderPath has "__pycache__"
    or FolderPath has_any (".hermes", ".openclaw", "agent_workspace")
| where InitiatingProcessFileName in~ ("python.exe", "node.exe", "uv.exe", "uvx.exe", "w3wp.exe", "java.exe")
| summarize Files = dcount(FolderPath), Sample = make_set(FileName, 8),
            Started = min(Timestamp), Ended = max(Timestamp)
    by DeviceId, DeviceName, InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessId, bin(Timestamp, 5m)
| where Files > 40
| extend BurstSeconds = datetime_diff('second', Ended, Started)
| order by Files desc
// TRIAGE: static site builds, report generators, config management and cache warming all
// look like this. Expect to allowlist several by InitiatingProcessFileName and folder before
// this is usable. Correlate a survivor against authentication activity from the same host in
// the same window; that pairing is what separates a build job from an operation.


// =========================================================================================
// 5. Failed-to-successful authentication transitions with concurrent parallel sessions.
//
// Unit 42 names "rapid 401/200 HTTP state shifts" and "parallel authentications". On its own
// the transition rate is a credential-spray signal your existing detections already carry.
//
// The JOIN is what is ours and what matters: multiple concurrent sessions on one credential
// performing DIFFERENT actions. Spray tools repeat one action widely. Agent swarms divide the
// work. Parallelism with divergence is the discriminator, not speed.
//
// HONESTY NOTE: this has not been measured, by us or by Unit 42, whose list is hunt guidance
// rather than telemetry. Section 3 of the paper is about exactly this and should be read
// before anyone treats the query as validated.
// =========================================================================================
SigninLogs
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
| extend Outcome = iff(ResultType == "0", "success", "failure")
| summarize
      Failures  = countif(Outcome == "failure"),
      Successes = countif(Outcome == "success"),
      DistinctApps = dcount(AppDisplayName),
      DistinctIPs  = dcount(IPAddress),
      Apps = make_set(AppDisplayName, 10)
    by UserPrincipalName, bin(TimeGenerated, 10m)
| where Failures > 5 and Successes > 0     // the transition
| where DistinctApps > 2                   // divergence: not one action repeated
| extend TransitionShape = strcat(Failures, " failed then ", Successes, " succeeded across ", DistinctApps, " apps")
| order by Failures desc
